# Implement {{title}} from its design folder

For Claude Code, or another coding agent, working in the full repository. The repository's root
`AGENTS.md` decides architecture, quality gates and the definition of done; it wins over anything
in this folder.

## Before you start

1. Run `{{statusCommand}}`. If the folder is stale, the project model changed after the design was
   made: review what changed, run `{{syncCommand}}` and check the ready rows still apply.
2. Read `handoff/implementation-map.md` and pick the rows with status `ready`. Rows with other
   statuses are not yours to build.
3. For each row, read its prototype files in `prototypes/`, the screen in `context/screens.md` and
   the decisions in `notes/decisions.md`.

## Implement

- Treat a prototype as a visual and behavioural reference, never as source to paste. Rebuild it with
  the project's components, composables and stores, and map its colours and spacing to the tokens in
  `context/design-tokens.md`. No inline styles, copied colour literals or remote assets.
- Map `data-design-id` attributes to the screen, component and interaction IDs of the project model,
  and keep those IDs in test selectors where the project's conventions allow it.
- A prototype that adds a screen, component or interaction changes the project model. Change the
  model first (for example with `node bin/app sketch`), regenerate, then `{{syncCommand}}`, so the
  canonical design and this folder stay in step.
- Business behaviour, persistence and permissions come from the project's requirements, not from
  prototype sample data.

## Verify and record

1. Run the gates named in the root `AGENTS.md` (at least `node bin/app check`), plus the UI checks
   for changed screens where the project has them (`node bin/app ui status`, browser tests).
2. Screenshots or a UI gallery are evidence for human review, never an accepted baseline.
3. Set the row to `implemented` with the commit or pull request. `verified` is set by a human after
   review.
